(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Treatment-resistant depression in bipolar disorder remains challenging. We) Tj
T*
(evaluated microbiota manipulation as an adjunct to pharmacotherapy. Methods: 35 adults) Tj
T*
(with bipolar disorder in depressive episodes were randomized to donor microbiota \(n=17\) or) Tj
T*
(autologous microbiota \(n=18\) transplantation. All participants continued their established) Tj
T*
(psychiatric medications. Results: Both treatment arms showed significant depression rating) Tj
T*
(score improvements from baseline to week 24 \(donor mean 16.7-point improvement vs) Tj
T*
(autologous 15.4-point improvement\). Statistical comparison revealed no significant) Tj
T*
(difference between groups \(t=-0.47, p=0.64, 95% CI -7.3 to 4.6\). Microbial sequencing) Tj
T*
(showed donor recipients achieved bacterial profiles resembling the healthy donor source) Tj
T*
(with increased microbial diversity by 6 months, whereas autologous recipients showed) Tj
T*
(minimal compositional change. No serious adverse events occurred in either group.) Tj
T*
(Conclusions: Both FMT approaches yielded clinically meaningful symptom reduction, though) Tj
T*
(microbial colonization differed. The absence of efficacy distinction between allogenic and) Tj
T*
(autologous approaches suggests need for inert control in future studies.) Tj
